Material modifications made by Cisco Systems, Inc.:

  - The detection engine was reimplemented in Go and absorbed into the
    DefenseClaw gateway sidecar. It is no longer a standalone Python sensor,
    a separate process, or a separately installed service.
  - The upstream official-build identity block -- the authorship module, the
    attribution digest and its verification, the CLI banner, and the
    "shadowclaw.*" telemetry resource attributes -- was removed. This build
    does not assert ShadowClaw project provenance and is not an official
    ShadowClaw release.
  - The Kinetic Trust Protocol (KTP) implementation, the KTP oracle sidecar,
    the hand-written OTLP exporter, the standalone tamper-evident ledger
    store, the redaction layer, and the macOS/Linux/Windows installer
    packaging were removed in favor of the existing DefenseClaw equivalents.
  - Cross-product corroboration against DefenseClaw's inventory JSON file was
    replaced by an in-process correlation join.

This NOTICE preserves the attribution required by Apache License 2.0 section 4.

Declared third-party dependencies are listed in go.mod/go.sum (Go) and
pyproject.toml/uv.lock (Python). The shipped OpenClaw TypeScript plugin is built
from extensions/defenseclaw/package.json and
extensions/defenseclaw/openclaw.plugin.json; the runtime archive carries them
as root package.json and openclaw.plugin.json files.
extensions/defenseclaw/package-lock.json is its locked build input but is not
placed in that runtime archive. The docs-site/package.json and
docs-site/package-lock.json files belong to the separately deployed
documentation site, not a DefenseClaw runtime artifact.

Redistributed contents vary by artifact. GoReleaser archive Syft SBOM sidecars
inventory the gateway archives. The Windows Setup merged SPDX 2.3 SBOM also
derives its exact linked Go module inventory from `go version -m`.

